---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href } from '../../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'releasenotes' as const;
---
<Base lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Release notes — Toolkit 1.x"
  description="What changed for you, release by release, written for the person who uses it.">
<div class="docs rn">
      <aside class="rn-side">
        <p class="grp">release line:</p>
        <div class="rn-line">
          <span class="rn-ln">Toolkit 1.x</span>
          <span class="rn-lv">1.0.0-beta.10</span>
          <span class="rn-ld">September 5, 2026 · 10 releases</span>
        </div>
        <p class="grp">on this page:</p>
        <ul class="rn-jump">
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-10" class="cur">1.0.0-beta.10</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-9">1.0.0-beta.9</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-8">1.0.0-beta.8</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-7">1.0.0-beta.7</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-6">1.0.0-beta.6</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-5">1.0.0-beta.5</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-4">1.0.0-beta.4</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-3">1.0.0-beta.3</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-2">1.0.0-beta.2</a></li>
          <li><a href="#v1-0-0-beta-1">1.0.0-beta.1</a></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="grp">also:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href={'https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md'} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Full changelog ↗</a></li>
          <li><a href={href('docsindex', 'en')}>Documentation</a></li>
        </ul>
      </aside>

      <div class="dmain">
        <div class="dhead stack g12">
          <p class="crumb"><b>release notes</b> / toolkit 1.x</p>
          <h1>Release notes</h1>
          <p class="lede measure">What changed for you, release by release — written for the person who uses it, not derived from commit messages. For the full record, with every constraint and edge case, see the changelog.</p>
          <div class="callout">
            <span class="ct">worth knowing before you read on</span>
            <p style="margin:0">The extension starts in <code>assist</code> mode: it helps and it flags, it never blocks a submission. The source of truth for conformance stays the server companion.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 class="rn-major">Version 1</h2>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-10">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.10</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 5, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.10" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">GitHub rewrote its “Files changed” view, and the extension had gone quiet on it: this release brings the whole toolkit back there, and makes diagnostics speak the language you picked.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>The comment tooling works again on GitHub's rewritten “Files changed” view.</strong> GitHub now serves that page at <code>/pull/N/changes</code>, where the extension recognised nothing: no label toolbar, no quick entry, no live validation, no badges on published comments — precisely where a blocking <code>issue:</code> counts most. All of it is back. A page carrying an editing surface the extension does not recognise now leaves a diagnostic entry too, instead of being inert and silent at once.</li>
            <li><strong>Under <code>enforce</code>, a non-compliant comment can no longer be published from that view.</strong> Neither by clicking <em>Reply</em> under a diff line, nor from the “Finish your comments” panel of a batch review, whose buttons sit in the overlay rather than beside the field. Only the keyboard path was guarded before, so a comment the extension had flagged could still be sent with a mouse.</li>
            <li><strong>Editing the root comment of a thread is validated again there.</strong> It was taken for a reply, which the default configuration exempts from validation — an <code>issue:</code> root could be edited into anything without the extension looking. Published comments get their badges back too, resolved threads read as resolved instead of unknown, and the pull request banner shows the authors and bodies it was missing.</li>
            <li><strong>A blocking label in a review summary is warned about again.</strong> In the “Finish your comments” body, <code>issue:</code> reported everything except the one warning that matters there: nobody can resolve a review summary, so it carries no blocking state.</li>
            <li><strong>The Preview tab shows badges on that view too.</strong> Writing <code>issue: …</code> in a composer on <code>…/changes</code> and switching to <em>Preview</em> rendered the prefix in plain text, where the same switch on the conversation page showed the badge.</li>
            <li><strong>The composer looks like itself again on that view.</strong> The toolbar sits above the field instead of being folded into a narrow column beside it, and the compliance outline around the field is visible instead of clipped. That spacing survives a trip through the <em>Preview</em> tab as well.</li>
            <li><strong>Diagnostic messages now follow the language you picked in the options page.</strong> A French interface could show <em>Conforme, avec avertissements</em> above <em>This comment is blocking but has no discussion</em>: that one line took its language from the repository's configuration rather than from your own preference. Label names and diagnostic codes stay untranslated in both languages — they are identifiers.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-9">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.9</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 4, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.9" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">The extension no longer gets in the way of a pull request's own description, no longer goes quiet on a page that starts out empty, and finally says why when it cannot read your configuration.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li>When the configuration cannot be read, the options page now shows <strong>why</strong> — <code>HTTP 429</code>, a <code>TypeError</code>, a 404 that cannot be told from a refusal — together with the level it came from, instead of the bare word <code>unreachable</code>. The reason stays on your machine.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>A pull request's own description is no longer treated as a review comment.</strong> The convention says nothing about it, yet the label toolbar was drawn on it — and under <code>enforce</code> the submit guard applied to its Update button, so a description without a <code>label:</code> prefix could not be saved at all.</li>
            <li><strong>The “Configuration unread” banner no longer appears on every repository.</strong> Every configuration read was failing before it left the browser, so the extension always fell back to its degraded state.</li>
            <li><strong>A page that had nothing to show no longer stops watching after five seconds.</strong> On <em>Files changed</em> with no thread yet, the first comment posted afterwards got neither badge nor banner until a full reload.</li>
            <li><strong>A missing page is no longer read as a hidden one while you are signed in.</strong> Two independent signals must now agree before a 404 means an unreadable configuration.</li>
            <li><strong>The selector degradation journal is usable again.</strong> Visiting a single closed pull request filled all fifty lines with one entry and evicted every real degradation.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-8">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.8</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 4, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.8" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">Published comments read like titles — badges, then the subject in bold on the same line — and three ways a comment could silently lose its badges are closed.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li>A published comment's subject is now shown <strong>in bold on the badge line</strong>, to the right of the badges. Where the subject cannot be delimited safely, the previous layout is kept rather than risking a wrong cut.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li>Editing a comment no longer strips its badges. The platform rewrites the rendered body on an update, and nothing brought them back until you reloaded.</li>
            <li>The structured prefix is re-hidden when the platform restores it on its own.</li>
            <li>Leaving a pull request, or switching to <code>mode: off</code>, no longer leaves part of a comment invisible — an extension that declares itself inactive went on concealing text you had written.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🔧 Changed</h4><ul>
            <li>The composer toolbar now stacks <strong>two rows</strong> — labels, then decorations — so a narrow window can no longer put a label button next to a decoration segment as if they were one kind of control.</li>
            <li>Label buttons no longer show their icon; badges on published comments still do. The buttons share one row's width, where an icon costs every other button.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-7">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.7</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 3, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.7" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">The structured prefix disappears from a published comment — the badges already say it — except everywhere saying it twice was the safer answer.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li><code>issue (blocking):</code> no longer appears in a published comment. On screen only — the stored comment is untouched, and reopening the edit form shows it in full.</li>
            <li>Where the badges would not say the same thing, the text stays: a decoration the configuration rejects, a case defect the tool exists to flag, decorations folded into a <code>+N</code> badge.</li>
            <li>A line that merely looks like a prefix — in a code block, a quote, a list, a heading, a table — is left alone.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🔧 Changed</h4><ul>
            <li>Every default label now has an icon and a colour, contrast-checked against WCAG 1.4.11.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-6">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.6</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 3, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.6" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">Decorations get badges of their own, and the whole display follows a configuration change while you watch.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li>Each decoration now gets its own badge beside the label: red where it forces blocking, green where it forces non-blocking, dashed where the decoration is declared nowhere.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li>Changing the configuration now refreshes what is already on screen, including on a tab left open on a pull request.</li>
            <li><strong>A comment box you already had open follows that change too</strong>, including whether it blocks your submission — an organisation switching from <code>enforce</code> to <code>off</code> left you blocked until you reopened the box.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-5">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.5</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 2, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.5" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">Repository configuration is readable again on private repositories.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li>The read follows a redirect the browser refuses to authenticate; credentials are now dropped only on that hop, so the file stays readable without asking you for any host permission.</li>
            <li>A configuration file unreadable for want of a session is no longer mistaken for a missing one — which silently applied the wrong configuration.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-4">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.4</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 1, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.4" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">The decoration selector says what your comment carries, not what you last clicked.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li>A decoration you removed by editing the text, or one that left with a label the configuration disabled, no longer stays lit in the toolbar.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-3">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.3</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">September 1, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.3" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">Two permissions dropped from the manifest, telemetry that exists only if you ask for it, and enough to submit to the stores.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🔧 Changed</h4><ul>
            <li><strong><code>activeTab</code> and the permanent host permission on <code>github.com</code> are gone.</strong> Hosts are granted on demand — the extension asks for what it needs, when it needs it.</li>
            <li>The pull request banner is a summary, not a merge barrier. The verdict belongs to the platform's check, already red and already blocking; replaying it here devalued both.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li>Optional telemetry, off until you turn it on, bound to the address you consented to and configured by your organization's policy rather than by a repository file.</li>
            <li>The version shown in <code>chrome://extensions</code> now distinguishes pre-releases.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>On GitHub Enterprise Server and self-hosted Azure DevOps Server, granting the host permission finally gives you the extension.</strong> The platform each host serves is now recorded with it.</li>
            <li><strong>An organization configuration hosted off your platform is readable again.</strong> It never had been: the page's CORS policy blocked it whatever permission was granted.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-2">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.2</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">August 24, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.2" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">Bot and tool comments stop being flagged, and the toolbar stops touching the edge.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li><strong>A tool command is no longer flagged as a malformed comment.</strong> A slash command, or a bot mention you list in <code>toolCommands</code>, is exempt — <code>/rebase</code> and <code>@dependabot recreate</code> pass through intact.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>🐛 Bug fixes</h4><ul>
            <li>The toolbar and the feedback align with the comment box's text instead of touching its border.</li>
            <li>A label's colour now paints the active button's border rather than its background: that colour comes from your configuration, and using it as a fill dropped contrast to 1.54 where 4.5 is required.</li>
            <li>Toolbar buttons are spaced again — a CSS comment closed one character early was silently swallowing the rule that spaced them.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>

        <article class="rel" id="v1-0-0-beta-1">
          <div class="rel-h"><span class="rel-v">1.0.0-beta.1</span><span class="rel-m">beta</span><span class="rel-d">August 24, 2026</span><a class="rel-tag" href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases/tag/v1.0.0-beta.1" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tag ↗</a></div>
          <p class="rel-sum">First installable release.</p>
          <div class="rel-s"><h4>✨ Features</h4><ul>
            <li>The extension for GitHub and Azure DevOps: a toolbar above the comment box, keyboard quick input, live feedback while typing, and a submit guard in <code>enforce</code> mode.</li>
            <li>The server companion, self-hostable, authoritative on conformance and on blocking a pull request's completion.</li>
            <li>Chromium and Firefox archives published on every tag, loadable without Node or npm.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
</Base>
